# Fieldset and legend

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A fieldset groups form controls that answer one question, such as the rooms a view includes or the day, month and year of a date. Its legend names the question. Each control still has its own label.

This page owns the two elements. The controls inside a fieldset are owned by [Checkbox](checkboxes.html), [Radios](radios.html) and [Date input](date-input.html).

This page covers:

- Fieldset and legend
  - `<fieldset>`
    - `<legend>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- The fieldset wraps the controls.
- The legend is the first child of the fieldset and names the group.
- An optional hint sits in its own element, after the legend.
- The controls, each with a visible label.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-fieldset"><code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code></h3>

A standard HTML element. It may contain one legend, as its first child, then any content. It accepts `disabled`, which disables the controls inside it except those in the legend, and `form` and `name`. A browser treats it as a group and takes its name from the legend.

Home Assistant has no fieldset element of its own. Use this one in Civica pages and in custom cards you write. Where Home Assistant draws a form itself, check the rendered accessibility tree before you add a wrapper of your own.

Browsers give a fieldset a grooved border, some padding, and a minimum width that fits its content. That minimum width can push a fieldset past the edge of a narrow screen, so set `min-width: 0` on it.

```html
<fieldset aria-describedby="rooms-hint">
  <legend>Rooms in this view</legend>
  <div id="rooms-hint">Select all that apply</div>
  <!-- checkboxes, each with its own label -->
</fieldset>
```

<h3 id="child-fieldset--legend"><code>&lt;legend&gt;</code></h3>

The title of the group. Put it first and keep it short, because some screen readers say it before each control in the group. Do not rely on every screen reader doing so. A heading may sit inside a legend: on a page with one question, the legend can hold the page heading; elsewhere use a lower heading level.

Keep the hint out of the legend. Put it in a separate element and link it to the fieldset with `aria-describedby`.

The Civica theme sets no variable for these two elements. Text uses `primary-text-color` (#0B0C0C in light mode, #FFFFFF in dark mode), hint text uses `secondary-text-color` (#484949 in light mode, #CBD0D3 in dark mode), and a border uses `divider-color` (#CECECE in light mode, #50565A in dark mode). The focus colour is #FFDD00.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The group name reaches people who cannot see the layout.
- A group stays clear without a border, so the border is optional.
- Nested fieldsets are allowed but quickly become hard to follow. Prefer one level.
- The default minimum width can overflow at 320 pixels.
- Screen readers differ in how often they repeat the legend.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a fieldset for room checkboxes, an exclusive display choice or the three parts of a date. Do not use it as a decorative box around unrelated settings. A set of sensor values is a [summary list](summary-list.html) or a table, not a form group.

In a dashboard editor, keep display choices apart from integration settings, so each legend describes one task.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the grouping is in the markup, not only in the layout.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: the legend and the hint say what to choose.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the group has a name.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the fieldset does not overflow at 320 pixels.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the group is not shown by colour alone.

One question for each group, with a short legend and a separate hint, keeps the load low.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Test the group name and each label with a screen reader and with a keyboard. Check that the group is clear without its border and that it fits a 320 pixel width. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

